{
  "identifier": "NG6519",
  "full_title": "The Finding of Moses",
  "short_title": "The Finding of Moses",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "baroque",
  "material": "oil on canvas",
  "dimensions": "115.7 × 175.3 cm",
  "production_date": "1651",
  "brief_description": "According to the Old Testament Book of Exodus, Pharaoh ordered all Israelite baby boys to be cast into the river, but Moses's mother hid him in a basket among the bulrushes. He was discovered there by Pharaoh's daughter, Thermutis, who is shown here dressed in a yellow robe and surrounded by her maidens. Moses is cradled by his sister, Miriam, dressed in white.\n\nThermutis decides to spare Moses from death and eventually adopts him. The joy of this event is reflected in Thermutis's welcoming smile and the excited gestures of her companions. Their idealised bodies and extravagant drapery reveal Poussin's interest in ancient sculpture. The palm trees and statue of a sphinx, a mythical creature with a human head and lion's body, tell us the scene is taking place in Egypt. Colourful clothing shimmers in the sunlight, conveying the warmth of the Egyptian climate.",
  "accession_date": "1988-01-01",
  "current_location": "Not on display, Amgueddfa Cymru — National Museum Wales",
